# Action creators — one per `Action` variant

## Context

`app/src/actions.ts` holds one creator per `Action` variant (`addTask`,
`toggleTask`, `removeTask`, `clearTasks`, `setTaskPriority`, `setFilter`) —
the sanctioned way to build `Action` objects.

## Rule

- Every new `Action` variant added to `app/src/types.ts` gets a matching
  named-export creator function in `app/src/actions.ts`, named
  `verbNoun`/`verb` (e.g. `addTask`, `setFilter`) — not `createAddTask` or
  `taskAddedAction`.
- Match the existing `type` string convention: `"<domain>/<lowercase verb>"`
  (`task/added`, `task/toggled`, `task/removed`, `task/cleared`,
  `task/prioritized`, `filter/set`), not `SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE` or a bare
  verb.
- Action creators only build and return an `Action` object — they never call
  `dispatch` themselves and never contain reducer logic (no branching on
  existing state).
- Consumers/tests dispatch via the creator (`store.dispatch(addTask(...))`),
  not by hand-writing `{ type: "...", payload: {...} }` inline.

## How to verify

1. Set equality between the `type` strings in the `Action` union
   (`app/src/types.ts`) and the `type` strings returned by creators in
   `app/src/actions.ts` — matching counts alone would miss a duplicated
   creator masking a missing one:
   ```bash
   diff <(grep -oE "['\"][a-z]+/[a-z]+['\"]" app/src/types.ts | tr -d "'\"" | sort -u) \
        <(grep -oE "['\"][a-z]+/[a-z]+['\"]" app/src/actions.ts | tr -d "'\"" | sort -u)
   ```
   No output means every `Action` variant has exactly one creator and there
   are no stray or duplicate `type` strings, whether quoted with `'` or `"`.
2. `grep -rnE "type[[:space:]]*:[[:space:]]*['\"]" app/src --include='*.ts'
   --include='*.tsx' --exclude='types.ts' --exclude='actions.ts'
   --exclude='reducer.ts' --exclude='*.test.ts' --exclude='*.test.tsx'
   --exclude='*.spec.ts' --exclude='*.spec.tsx'`
   returns no matches — nothing builds raw action objects by hand elsewhere,
   whether quoted with `'` or `"` or spaced around the colon.
3. New `type` strings match `^[a-z]+\/[a-z]+$` and read as a lowercase verb
   (`added`/`removed`/`toggled`/`cleared`/`prioritized`/`set`), consistent
   with the existing six.
4. `cd app && npm test` passes, including a test exercising the new creator.